course: dictionary
lesson: 14778
title: Provoke: to annoy or to cause
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To provoke someone is to annoy them on purpose, so they get angry or react. «Ben kept poking his sister just to provoke her.» Ben wants his sister to react, and poking is how he tries to make it happen.
explain: Provoke can also mean to cause a feeling or a reaction. «The surprising ending provoked gasps from the whole class.» Nobody was trying to annoy anyone here. The ending caused the gasps. Ask yourself: is someone trying to make a reaction happen, or did something simply cause one?
explain: With animals, provoke means to tease or bother them so they may attack. That is why you should never provoke a dog, snake, bee or wild animal. If someone in the playground tries to provoke you, walk away and tell an adult.
fact: Provoke means to annoy someone on purpose, or to cause a reaction or feeling.
fact: Provoke is a verb: provokes, provoked, provoking.
fact: «Easily provoked» means someone gets angry or upset very quickly.
fact: «Provoke a reaction» means to cause a reaction, such as laughing, gasping or arguing.
fact: Never provoke a dog, snake, bee or other wild animal, because it may attack you.
exercise: «The noisy group tried to provoke the new boy by pushing his bag.» What does provoke mean here? | To make him fall asleep | *To annoy him on purpose | To help him carry his bag | To thank him
exercise: «The mysterious noise provoked a lot of whispers in the class.» What does provoked mean here? | Forgot | *Caused | Hid | Stopped
exercise: What should you do if someone tries to provoke you in the playground? | Shout back and push them | *Walk away and tell an adult | Hide your bag | Laugh loudly at them
test: «Her easily provoked brother shouted at the smallest joke.» What does easily provoked mean? | Very tired and sleepy | Never speaks | Happy all day | *Gets angry very quickly
test: Why should you never provoke a wild animal? | Because it will give you a present | *Because it may attack you | Because it likes to be teased | Because it will go to sleep
exam: «The new rule provoked a long debate among the parents.» In this sentence, provoked means | *Caused | Forgot | Tidied | Sang
